  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 239 AM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe flow aloft will become more WSW today as best monsoon \nmoisture shifts mainly across srn portions of the area. Meanwhile,\na secondary surge of drier air in the lower levels will move \nacross the plains. By aftn, MLCAPE will range between 500-800 j/kg\nand be confined to the srn foothills, Palmer Divide and over sern\nportions of the CWA. With decent shear in place, can't rule out a\nfew stronger storms in this area by late aftn thru the early \nevening hours. PW values will still be around 150 percent of \nnormal, in the same general area, so may see locally heavy \nrainfall as well. Over the rest of the area, shower and tstms will\nbe more isolated in coverage. Highs today will drop back into the\nmid to upper 80's across the plains.\n\nFor Wed, drier in westerly flow aloft will be over the area as a \ncold front move across.  As a result, expect dry conditions with \ncooler temps as highs finally drop back to more seasonal levels.\nBy Wed night, low level southeast flow will develop which will lead \nto some return of moisture across the plains.  Meanwhile, a weak \ndisturbance embedded in WNW flow aloft may bring some nocturnal \ntstms to portions of the sern plains overnight.  Current data, keeps \nmost of this activity over sern CO with srn Lincoln county being on \nthe nrn fringe. For now, have kept in chc pops mainly across srn \nportions of Lincoln county.\n\nOn Thu, the flow aloft will remain WNW as southerly level flow is \nacross the plains.  MLCAPE will rise up to 1000 j/kg over portions \nof the plains by aftn.  For now, chances for tstms look on the low \nside as I can't find a focus for activity, however, that doesn't \nmean there couldn't be an isold storm or two across the plains by \nlate aftn. Meanwhile, temps will begin to warm as readings reach \nthe upper 80's to lower 90's over nern CO.\n\nLooking ahead to Fri, zonal flow aloft will be over the area as a \nsfc lee trough develops over the plains.  This will lead to another \nwarm day as highs rise into the upper 80's to mid 90's over the \nplains.  Meanwhile, models have some mid level moisture embedded in \nthe flow, which may lead to a slight chc of higher based \nshowers/tstms over the higer terrain.  Across the plains, MLCAPE is \nrather minimal so chances of precip look low.\n\nFor Fri night into Sat, zonal flow aloft will continue.  Meanwhile, \na cold front will move across Fri night into Sat morning.  This \nfront will drop temps back to more seasonal levels across nern CO. \nOnce again MLCAPE is rather minimal and confined to the higher \nterrain.  Thus will keep in a slight chc for showers/tstms \nmainly across the mtns.\n\nBy Sun, an upper level trough will move into the nrn Rockies with \nincreasing WSW flow aloft across the area.  At the sfc, a lee trough \nwill intensify east of the mtns with southerly low level flow across \nthe plains.  MLCAPE is fcst to increase to around 1000 j/kg over the \nplains east of a pseudo dryline.  Thus may see a few stronger storms \nby late aftn thru the evening hours.  Highs on Sun, will likely rise \nback to above normal levels.\n\nLooking ahead to Mon, rather dry WSW flow aloft will be over the \nareas a cool front affects the plains.  Latest data, doesn't show \nmuch low level moisture behind the front so it may end up dry with \ntemps dropping back to seasonal normals.",
